    "description": "The Sefidrud connects the Qezel Uzan and Shahrud catchments with the Caspian lowlands of Gilan. Its mountain passage, regulated water supply and sediment transport help explain both the region’s agricultural landscape and its river-management challenges. [s1][s2][s3]",
    "tags": ["Sefidrud", "Gilan", "Caspian Sea", "Manjil", "Sediment management", "Irrigation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T14:08:36Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T14:08:36Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Sefidrud River: Manjil, Gilan and the Caspian Lowlands | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Follow the Sefidrud from its major tributaries through Manjil to Gilan, and understand its irrigation role, sediment problems and competing water demands."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "river-and-corridor",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A passage from the interior to the Caspian",
        "content": "The Sefidrud, also written Safidrud, connects Iran’s northwestern interior with the Caspian lowlands of Gilan. Its name means “White River.” The upstream Qezel Uzan meets the Shahrud near Manjil; below that meeting, the Sefidrud passes through the Alborz mountain barrier toward the coastal plain. This helps explain why descriptions of the named lower river and of the entire tributary system may cover different geographical extents. [s1]\n\nThe valley is more than a route for water. Encyclopaedia Iranica describes the Manjil passage as a corridor between inland and Caspian environments, associated with distinctive winds and an ecological transition toward the humid lowlands. Farther downstream, river deposits helped form the agricultural landscape of central Gilan. The river therefore connects mountain geology, lowland soils and human settlement within a single geographical system. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "sediment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "The reservoir stores sediment as well as water",
        "content": "The reservoir at Manjil is central to understanding the regulated river. Sediment arriving from upstream settles in storage, progressively occupying space that would otherwise hold water. A 2017 engineering paper on the Sefidrud examines this problem and methods of removing accumulated deposits. Its discussion links sedimentation with losses of storage and difficulties for irrigation and hydropower infrastructure. [s2]\n\nOne technique is flushing: water is released through low outlets so that flowing water erodes and carries deposited material downstream. The paper examines regressive erosion, in which an erosional channel develops back into the sediment deposit. This is a physical intervention in stored material, rather than simply releasing clear reservoir water. Its effectiveness depends on hydraulic conditions and the arrangement of deposits. [s2]\n\nThe engineering study supports an explanation of the mechanism, but it does not establish today’s remaining storage or the ecological consequences of a particular release. Those questions require dated operational measurements and downstream observations. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "competing-demands",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Why management must extend across the basin",
        "content": "The Japan International Cooperation Agency’s description of its Sefidrud basin study identifies demands for irrigation, industry and domestic supply alongside the need to maintain ecosystems, wetlands and fisheries. It warns that the aggregate water volumes envisaged in development plans exceeded natural river flow. This describes a planning problem identified by that study; it is not a newly calculated water balance for the editorial cutoff. [s3]\n\nThe implication is that projects cannot be assessed only at their individual sites. A proposed withdrawal may appear feasible locally while reducing what remains available elsewhere in the connected system. JICA accordingly presents integrated water-resources management as a basin-wide task requiring attention to competing uses and environmental needs. [s3]\n\nFor readers discovering Gilan, the Sefidrud offers a useful connection between landscape and infrastructure. Its lowland agricultural importance rests on water arriving through a much larger upstream network, while the reservoir introduces continuing questions about storage, sediment and allocation. Understanding those relationships is more informative than treating the river, dam and coastal plain as separate attractions. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "river-connections",
        "type": "markdown-mermaid",
        "title": "The Sefidrud’s principal geographical connections",
        "content": "```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n  A[\"Qezel Uzan\"] -->|\"Flows into the confluence at Manjil\"| C[\"Manjil reservoir and dam\"]\n  B[\"Shahrud\"] -->|\"Flows into the confluence at Manjil\"| C\n  C -->|\"Releases water downstream\"| D[\"Lower Sefidrud\"]\n  D -->|\"Flows through\"| E[\"Gilan lowlands and delta\"]\n  E -->|\"River discharge reaches\"| F[\"Caspian Sea\"]\n```",
        "summary": "The arrows show the principal downstream water connections. This simplified diagram omits smaller tributaries and irrigation diversions and does not quantify releases or imply unrestricted flow.",
        "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      }
    ],
